  This README file contains information on the following:
  
   1 Installation
   2 The utility programs
   3 KLIC
   4 Electronic caveats
  
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  1  INSTALLATION
  Complete installation (all the data, programs, etc.) requires approximately
  8 MB of free disk space. The installation routine quits when the available
  space is less than 10k. For instructions on installing the electronic files,
  see page 201-2 of the book, where sizes of individual parts of the data are given.
  For the sizes of (uncompressed) individual files see CHECK.LST on this disk.
  
  If you are installing from drive b: add the letter b after a space at the
  end of the command line, thus:
  
      b:instlmo2 b
  
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  2  The UTILITY PROGRAMS
  Discovering the limitations, quirks, and full potential of the programs
  supplied with these data will require patience and curiosity and experi-
  mentation. While preparing the texts of these volumes, editing the data,
  and writing the programs, there has been little time for systematic
  exploration and use of the programs, other than in purely formal testing.
   Nevertheless, in the weeks between sending the text to the press
  and while finalizing the electronic material, I have been able to devote
  a week or two to the kinds of questions and uses for which the programs
  might be useful. As a result, with respect to both data and utility pro-
  grams I have implemented a few improvements and new features that are not
  documented in the printed text. None of these will make the data or
  programs more difficult to use, and most will be completely invisible
  to the reader who does not study the addition information in the READ-ME
  files in this directory on disk 1.
   Two files are provided. Each is in ASCII format and in the format
  used by WordPerfect 5.1.
   CH-READ (.ME or .WP5) first lists the nearly 70 rhymed offices
  for which encoded chants are provided (see Handlist A). It then describes
  refinements to chant encoding and some alternatives for indexing with
  WordCruncher. Finally, it presents a very concise summary of the process
  of chant encoding that could be printed on an index card for easy reference.
   CHNTSRCH (.DOC or .WP5) describes some enhancements to the utility
  program provided for use with the chant data: included in the new features
  is the ability to search the data for purely textual as well as chant
  material (see also the next section here, referring to KLIC).   
  
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  3  KLIC
  KLIC is a concordance program developed for LMLO: Texts. Since the chant
  files included with LMLO: Sources and Chants are text files with additional
  lines of data for the chant encoding, KLIC is potentially of use with the
  chant data.
  
  Even though the program is not described or mentioned in LMLO: Sources and
  Chants, I have included it in the \PROGRAMS subdirectory. A few
  enhancements have been added to the version provided with LMLO: Texts. For a
  full description of its power, readers must consult LMLO: Texts.
  
  Briefly, it will provide a context for the string specified on the command
  line, finding EXACT matches. Thus to find this exact sequence of characters
  in a chantword - .23$.43=1, - the command would be
   Snorun KLIC /y=filename(s) /f=".23$.43=1," /nq
  
  One advantage over ChntSrch is KLIC's ability to search for strings
  with wildcards.
  
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  4  ELECTRONIC CAVEATS
  Users even slightly familar with computers will recognize that software
  is seldom definitive. The number of possible ways of using even relatively
  simple programs is inordinately large (even discounting
  unforeseeable, imprudent, or reckless combinations and permutations) and
  quite impossible to take into consideration or to test rigorously.
  
  The software provided free with this book is tailormade for the repertory.
  Like all software, it will certainly have bugs. I am of course anxious to
  learn of defects and to hear of possible improvements. First, however,
  users should make sure they have carefully read all the instructions and
  that they fully understand the structure of the data. I cannot spend
  unreasonable amounts of time attempting to diagnose bugs. If users cannot
  achieve a particular end easily with the software provided, they may find
  it useful to explore commercial programs to carry out the task required.
  
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  